class:: BarkBands
summary:: Bark spaced triangular spectral bands
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/MFCC2

description::
Traunmuller (1990) Bark mapping: z(f) = 26.81 * f / (1960 + f) - 0.53, with f in Hz. MIR2 uses the uncorrected rational mapping over the selected range; no low/high Bark correction is silently applied. Source: link::https://doi.org/10.1121/1.399849::.

The bank places numBands+2 equally spaced points on that named scale, converts them to Hz, and builds piecewise-linear triangular filters in Hz. Each output is sum(weight * spectrum). Normalized mode divides each filter by the sum of its sampled FFT-bin weights. Filters with no sampled support return zero; increase fftSize or reduce numBands if low-frequency filters are narrower than the FFT resolution. Power mode squares the raw FFT magnitude before filtering. Silence returns zero.

All configuration except chain is read at construction. The weight matrix occupies 8 * numBands * (fftSize/2+1) bytes and processing is O(numBands * fftSize) per frame, with no runtime allocation or extra lookahead. A 1024-sample FFT window is 21.3 ms at 48 kHz.

Vanilla link::Classes/MFCC:: combines Mel bands and a cepstral transform; BarkBands exposes its Bark bands directly for musical control, without a DCT.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain with a buffer matching fftSize.
argument:: numBands
Literal integer 4..128. Output count.
argument:: minFreq
Lower filterbank edge in Hz. Default 20.
argument:: maxFreq
Upper filterbank edge in Hz. Zero selects Nyquist.
argument:: normalize
Positive gives a discrete weighted mean per band; 0 gives a weighted sum.
argument:: spectrumMode
Literal 0 magnitude or 1 power. Default 1.
argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two 256..8192, matching the FFT buffer.
returns::
One control per band in ascending center-frequency order.

examples::
BarkBands separates spectrum into psychoacoustic critical bands based on the Traunmuller Bark scale. In this example, it drives an adaptive multiband transient exciter: low critical bands receive warm saturation while high bands trigger crisp harmonic distortion, enhancing punch across dynamic drum grooves. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.bars:: displays the real-time energy distribution across all 16 critical bands.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("drums.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 16);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic drum fallback
            var trig = Impulse.kr(2);
            var kick = SinOsc.ar(EnvGen.ar(Env([120, 45], [0.08], \exp), trig)) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.3), trig);
            var snare = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.15), Impulse.kr(2, 0.5));
            (kick + snare) * 0.5
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, hop: 0.5);
        var bands = BarkBands.kr(chain, numBands: 16, minFreq: 50, maxFreq: 12000, fftSize: 1024);
        var normBands = (bands / (bands.sum + 1e-6)).lag(0.03);

        // Low critical bands drive warm saturation
        var lowDrive = (source * (1.0 + (normBands[0..3].sum * 8))).tanh * 0.25;
        // High critical bands drive high-frequency bite
        var highDrive = HPF.ar(source, 3000) * (normBands[10..15].sum * 12).clip(0, 4);
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + lowDrive + (highDrive * 0.15);

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, normBands) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.bars("Bark Critical Bands", ~mir2Bus, 16, 0.0, 0.5);
});
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

